  "outcome": "pending_approval",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Tom, I found it. Harborview Supplies' invoice #4471 for the October pallets arrived this morning, but our spam filter held it back. It comes from Harborview's normal billing address, so I've asked for it to be released. Once a technician approves that, it will show up in your inbox. Please reply to let us know when you have it. One more thing: your account is currently locked after 5 wrong password attempts this morning on an office computer. If you're having trouble signing in, just contact the help desk and we'll get it unlocked. Also, a separate message called \"Scanned document 0931.zip\" from an unknown sender was held because it contained malware. It will stay blocked, so please don't ask the sender to send it again.",
  "internal_note": "Followed KB-007. Quarantine for tom.becker has Q-1004 from billing@harborview-supplies.example, subject 'Invoice #4471 - October pallets', received 2026-10-09 12:21 UTC, held as spam and marked releasable. The sender domain matches the vendor the user named, and I saw no look-alike characters. The user's ticket shows he was expecting this email, so I queued release_email for Q-1004 (approval id 1). Q-1005 (scanner@docs-share.example, 0931.zip) is held as malware and is not releasable. I did not release it; it's on file for KB-008 awareness. Side finding: the account is locked (failed_login_count 5). The 5 failed_password attempts were 2026-10-09 09:41-09:56 ET from corporate IP 203.0.113.10 in New York, inside usual hours. That's his normal pattern, and all earlier sign-ins succeeded with Duo. This looks like the user mistyping his password, not a compromise. The user didn't ask for an unlock, so none is queued; I can queue unlock_account if he asks. After approval, check that the invoice reached his inbox.",
